        'Into that executive vacuum steps judicial improvisation. Environmental governance in Jammu and Kashmir increasingly moves only when a citizen petitioner or legal scholar carries published academic papers before the National Green Tribunal (NGT) or the High Court via Public Interest Litigation (Sharma & Saxena, 2026; Jafri et al., 2025). While judicial intervention has repeatedly rescued wetlands and riverbeds from total neglect, governing an entire mountain biosphere through episodic courtroom litigation is neither scalable nor preventative; courts can issue interim penalties after damage is done, whereas only statutory executive triggers can prevent contamination continuously.',
